00:03:31.355 --> 00:03:32.208
Part 1.

42
00:03:32.488 --> 00:03:34.408
Introduction and Why It Matters.

43
00:03:35.658 --> 00:03:37.877
A story: the timeout that paid twice.

44
00:03:38.157 --> 00:03:42.466
At 2:10 pm a beneficiary bank's UPI systems slow down.

45
00:03:42.746 --> 00:03:51.023
The remitter bank has already debited its customers' accounts, sent the credit requests through the UPI switch, and is now receiving timeouts.

46
00:03:51.303 --> 00:03:52.413
The first fix.

47
00:03:52.693 --> 00:03:56.106
An engineer adds a rule: on timeout, reverse the debit.

48
00:03:56.386 --> 00:03:59.799
Customers get their money back immediately, and complaints stop.

49
00:04:00.079 --> 00:04:01.189
The morning after.

50
00:04:01.469 --> 00:04:07.186
The settlement file shows that more than half of those failed payments were in fact credited to the beneficiary.

51
00:04:07.466 --> 00:04:14.698
The bank has paid tens of thousands of payments twice, and must now recover money from customers who believe they did nothing wrong.

52
00:04:16.048 --> 00:04:17.115
Rolling it back.

53
00:04:17.395 --> 00:04:20.339
The rule is reversed to treat timeouts as successful.

54
00:04:20.619 --> 00:04:25.781
At the next incident, customers whose payments really failed wait a day or more for their money.

55
00:04:26.061 --> 00:04:31.523
Some go past the regulator's turnaround time, and the bank owes compensation (VERIFY:

56
00:04:31.803 --> 00:04:35.024
RBI's TAT framework for failed transactions).

57
00:04:35.304 --> 00:04:37.736
Both rules were simple, and both were wrong.

58
00:04:38.016 --> 00:04:43.968
The correct answer is a state machine with status checks, settlement-driven resolution and a deadline.

59
00:04:44.248 --> 00:04:51.032
Banking is full of these: money that must never be created or lost, even when the systems holding it disagree.

60
00:04:52.382 --> 00:04:53.065
Quote:

61
00:04:53.345 --> 00:04:55.009
The thesis of this chapter.

62
00:04:55.289 --> 00:04:57.273
A bank is a ledger with deadlines.

63
00:04:57.553 --> 00:05:01.713
Every rupee is in exactly one account at every moment (double entry).

64
00:05:01.993 --> 00:05:05.107
Every state change is recorded and never overwritten.

65
00:05:05.387 --> 00:05:08.694
Hot accounts are fed by an append-only journal (L1).

66
00:05:08.974 --> 00:05:13.795
Unknown outcomes are resolved by status and settlement, not guesses (L2).

67
00:05:14.075 --> 00:05:17.446
Arithmetic follows the product terms to the paisa (L3).

68
00:05:17.726 --> 00:05:21.651
The payment graph is watched for money that moves like fraud (L4).

69
00:05:23.001 --> 00:05:24.623
Core banking in one picture.

70
00:05:24.903 --> 00:05:26.268
Here is a quick table:

71
00:05:26.548 --> 00:05:27.849
Concept, and Meaning.

72
00:05:28.129 --> 00:05:29.537
Customer (CIF):

73
00:05:29.817 --> 00:05:32.761
The customer information file, linked to KYC.

74
00:05:33.041 --> 00:05:33.895
Account:

75
00:05:34.175 --> 00:05:40.233
Savings, current, loan, fixed deposit; a product defines its rules (interest, limits, fees).

76
00:05:40.513 --> 00:05:41.623
Ledger balance:

77
00:05:41.903 --> 00:05:43.439
The sum of posted entries.

78
00:05:43.719 --> 00:05:44.913
Available balance:

79
00:05:45.193 --> 00:05:49.289
Ledger balance minus holds (liens) + any overdraft limit.

80
00:05:49.569 --> 00:05:50.700
Hold or lien:

81
00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:58.020
Money reserved for a pending debit (a card authorisation, a UPI debit awaiting the outcome, a court order).

82
00:05:58.300 --> 00:05:59.132
Posting:

83
00:05:59.412 --> 00:06:03.252
A balanced set of entries (debits = credits) with a value date.

84
00:06:04.602 --> 00:06:06.245
General ledger (GL):

85
00:06:06.525 --> 00:06:13.991
Bank-level accounts (for example, UPI settlement receivable, interest expense) that customer postings roll into.

86
00:06:14.271 --> 00:06:15.295
EOD or BOD:

87
00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:22.957
End- and beginning-of-day processing: interest accrual, charges, standing instructions, statements, GL balancing.

88
00:06:24.307 --> 00:06:25.629
Learning objectives.

89
00:06:25.909 --> 00:06:27.787
By the end you will be able to:

90
00:06:28.067 --> 00:06:31.971
Model accounts, holds and double-entry postings with value dates.

91
00:06:32.251 --> 00:06:35.579
Scale hot accounts without breaking the no-overdraft rule.

92
00:06:35.859 --> 00:06:40.147
Handle UPI timeouts with a state machine that meets turnaround times.

93
00:06:40.427 --> 00:06:44.075
Compute interest and fees exactly as the product terms say.

94
00:06:44.355 --> 00:06:48.152
Detect mule accounts using time-windowed, graph-aware features.

95
00:06:49.502 --> 00:06:50.377
Part 2.

96
00:06:50.657 --> 00:06:52.555
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

97
00:06:53.805 --> 00:06:56.109
Intuition: a very careful notebook.

98
00:06:56.389 --> 00:07:07.824
A bank's core system is a notebook where every line moves money from one page to another, the two sides always add up, and nobody ever erases a line: mistakes are fixed with new lines.

99
00:07:08.104 --> 00:07:11.155
The balance on each page is just the sum of its lines.

100
00:07:11.435 --> 00:07:21.355
Everything else (apps, UPI, cards, branches) is a way of asking the notebook to add lines, and every one of them must be answered yes, written or no, not written.

101
00:07:21.635 --> 00:07:23.725
Maybe must never be the final answer.

102
00:07:25.075 --> 00:07:26.035
Requirements.

103
00:07:26.315 --> 00:07:27.681
Here is a quick table:

104
00:07:27.961 --> 00:07:29.262
Type, and Requirement.

105
00:07:29.542 --> 00:07:30.417
Functional:

106
00:07:30.697 --> 00:07:44.158
Open accounts (with KYC); deposits, withdrawals and transfers (IMPS, NEFT, RTGS, UPI); holds; interest and fees; statements; loans and EMIs; standing instructions; disputes.

107
00:07:44.438 --> 00:07:45.697
Scale (assumptions):

108
00:07:45.977 --> 00:08:00.547
50M accounts; UPI peak of a few thousand transactions per second for a large bank, much more for the switch as a whole (VERIFY current NPCI volumes); a few merchant accounts receiving hundreds of credits per second.

109
00:08:00.827 --> 00:08:01.745
Correctness:

110
00:08:02.025 --> 00:08:10.963
Double entry always balanced; no overdraft beyond the limit; exactly-once posting per request; interest exactly per product terms.

111
00:08:11.243 --> 00:08:12.310
Availability:

112
00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:17.753
UPI and cards 24 times 7, with no EOD downtime for payments.

113
00:08:18.033 --> 00:08:18.929
Deadlines:

114
00:08:19.209 --> 00:08:25.459
Failed-transaction reversals within regulator TATs, with compensation otherwise (VERIFY).

115
00:08:26.809 --> 00:08:27.748
Compliance:

116
00:08:28.028 --> 00:08:35.836
KYC or AML, transaction monitoring, payment data stored in India (VERIFY RBI storage directive), audit trails.

117
00:08:37.186 --> 00:08:39.170
Postings, holds and balances.

118
00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:52.271
A posting is a set of entries that sum to zero: a debit to the customer's account;. a credit to the UPI settlement GL;. and for fees, lines to the fee income GL and a GST GL.

119
00:08:52.551 --> 00:08:53.959
Entries are immutable.

120
00:08:54.239 --> 00:08:58.271
A correction is a reversing posting that references the original.

121
00:08:59.621 --> 00:09:01.584
Holds separate intent from movement.

122
00:09:01.864 --> 00:09:07.923
A UPI debit or card authorisation first places a hold, which reduces available balance.

123
00:09:08.203 --> 00:09:11.552
The hold is then either converted to a posting or released.

124
00:09:11.832 --> 00:09:14.115
Holds have expiry times and reasons.

125
00:09:14.395 --> 00:09:15.525
Idempotency.

126
00:09:15.805 --> 00:09:27.816
Every posting carries the upstream reference (for UPI, the transaction's reference id) with a unique constraint, so a retried request returns the original result (Ch 20).

127
00:09:28.096 --> 00:09:33.429
Value date versus posting date: interest is computed on value-dated balances.

128
00:09:33.709 --> 00:09:37.869
A back-dated correction changes interest, which needs recomputation.

129
00:09:39.219 --> 00:09:40.201
Hot accounts.

130
00:09:40.481 --> 00:09:46.625
A large merchant's or aggregator's settlement account can receive hundreds or thousands of credits per second.

131
00:09:46.905 --> 00:09:51.854
Updating one balance row serialises all of them on one row lock (L1:

132
00:09:52.134 --> 00:09:59.387
1,585 txn per second, p50 6.7 ms with 16 clients).

133
00:09:59.667 --> 00:10:04.233
Credits are safe to decouple: adding money can never break the no-overdraft rule.

134
00:10:04.513 --> 00:10:11.979
Append-only journal: each credit is an insert, and a roll-up folds committed rows into the balance every few hundred ms.

135
00:10:12.259 --> 00:10:13.091
L1:

136
00:10:13.371 --> 00:10:16.123
5,687 per second.

137
00:10:16.403 --> 00:10:19.646
Sub-accounts: credits are spread across N balance rows.

138
00:10:19.926 --> 00:10:20.758
L1:

139
00:10:21.038 --> 00:10:23.641
3,932 per second.

140
00:10:24.991 --> 00:10:26.719
Debits must see the whole balance.

141
00:10:26.999 --> 00:10:35.084
They take a per-account lock and check the effective balance: rolled-up balance plus unrolled journal rows, or the sum of the sub-accounts.

142
00:10:35.364 --> 00:10:44.687
In a refund storm, debits dominate and all designs converge (L1: ~2,450 per second at 30% debits).

143
00:10:44.967 --> 00:10:46.311
The sequence-gap trap:

144
00:10:46.591 --> 00:10:54.868
Rolling up everything below sequence N skips rows whose transactions haven't committed yet, because sequences are assigned before commit.

145
00:10:55.148 --> 00:10:59.436
L1 marks rows as rolled instead, which only sees committed rows.

146
00:11:00.786 --> 00:11:10.194
Alternatives: roll up rows older than the longest possible transaction, or use the database's commit-order facilities (VERIFY for your database).

147
00:11:10.474 --> 00:11:14.485
The display balance for a hot account may lag by the roll-up interval.

148
00:11:14.765 --> 00:11:16.258
The debit check never does.

149
00:11:17.608 --> 00:11:19.741
UPI: four parties and one truth.

150
00:11:20.021 --> 00:11:34.677
The parties (P2M example): the payer's app (payer PSP);. the UPI switch (NPCI);. the payer's bank (remitter), which debits;. the beneficiary's bank, which credits;. plus the merchant's PSP or aggregator.

151
00:11:34.957 --> 00:11:39.544
The simplified flow (VERIFY against the current NPCI specifications):

152
00:11:40.894 --> 00:11:44.222
Collect or intent, then PIN on the payer's device.

153
00:11:44.502 --> 00:11:48.065
Debit request to the remitter bank, which debits and responds.

154
00:11:48.345 --> 00:11:52.121
Credit request to the beneficiary bank, which credits and responds.

155
00:11:52.401 --> 00:11:54.491
Final status to both PSPs.

156
00:11:54.771 --> 00:11:58.718
Net settlement between banks happens in cycles, with settlement files.

157
00:11:58.998 --> 00:12:02.177
Where it breaks: any hop can time out after doing its work.

158
00:12:02.457 --> 00:12:10.414
The most dangerous case is the one this chapter's story is about: the remitter has debited but doesn't know whether the beneficiary credited.

159
00:12:11.764 --> 00:12:14.324
The design rule: no participant guesses.

160
00:12:14.604 --> 00:12:28.663
Each has: a state machine;. a status-check operation keyed by the transaction reference;. a reconciliation against settlement files;. a deadline: reversal within the regulator's TAT, or compensation (VERIFY).

161
00:12:30.013 --> 00:12:31.847
Resolving unknown outcomes.

162
00:12:32.127 --> 00:12:33.493
Here is a quick table:

163
00:12:33.773 --> 00:12:35.671
Policy, and What L2 showed.

164
00:12:35.951 --> 00:12:37.637
R1 reverse immediately:

165
00:12:37.917 --> 00:12:44.722
60,381 paid twice: the beneficiary had credited, and the payer was refunded too.

166
00:12:45.002 --> 00:12:47.455
Recovery is painful and customer-hostile.

167
00:12:47.735 --> 00:12:49.954
R2 treat as success, fix later:

168
00:12:50.234 --> 00:12:58.810
49,494 payers short for more than 24 h, all failed payments waiting for the T+1 batch.

169
00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:02.013
R3 status checks with backoff, then decide:

170
00:13:02.293 --> 00:13:03.957
Most resolved within minutes.

171
00:13:04.237 --> 00:13:13.495
Those still unknown after the checks (the beneficiary's status API was down during its 3.5 h incident) fall to T+1:

172
00:13:13.775 --> 00:13:17.850
19,530 short for more than 24 h.

173
00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:21.501
R4 R3, plus reverse at the next settlement cycle:

174
00:13:21.781 --> 00:13:26.773
0 paid twice, 0 short for more than 24 h, no manual cases.

175
00:13:27.053 --> 00:13:35.565
22,593 were short for more than 1 h: those waiting for the settlement cycle after a long incident.

176
00:13:36.915 --> 00:13:38.237
Engineering details:

177
00:13:38.517 --> 00:13:41.653
Hold the debit as PENDING (not reversed, not final).

178
00:13:41.933 --> 00:13:46.925
Schedule status checks at growing intervals (Ch 18) and budget their load:

179
00:13:47.205 --> 00:13:53.243
R3 or R4 made 705k calls, concentrated at incident recovery.

180
00:13:53.523 --> 00:13:54.440
Add jitter.

181
00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:58.880
Consume settlement and reconciliation files as the source of truth.

182
00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:03.512
Track every PENDING against the TAT deadline, with alerts well before it.

183
00:14:04.862 --> 00:14:06.761
Interest, fees and the paisa.

184
00:14:07.041 --> 00:14:23.489
Product terms define the arithmetic: rate, day-count convention (actual or 365, actual or actual, 30 or 360), accrual frequency, rounding and credit frequency (VERIFY RBI rules for savings interest).

185
00:14:23.769 --> 00:14:25.454
L3 on a million accounts:

186
00:14:25.734 --> 00:14:32.113
Daily rounding looks harmless in aggregate: +306 rupeeson 95 crore rupees.

187
00:14:32.393 --> 00:14:40.819
But it changes 977k accounts by up to 0.45, rupeesso no account matches the product terms.

188
00:14:41.099 --> 00:14:47.670
Truncation systematically underpays: minus 4.38 lakh rupees a quarter, a conduct risk.

189
00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:53.052
The wrong day count in a leap year overpays 26 lakh rupees.

190
00:14:53.332 --> 00:15:05.876
The rules: accrue at high precision (a decimal type, never floating point);. round once at posting, as the terms specify;. store the accrual basis for audit;. test against published examples.

191
00:15:07.226 --> 00:15:13.989
EOD at 24 times 7: accrual runs against a consistent snapshot of value-dated balances.

192
00:15:14.269 --> 00:15:21.181
It must not freeze payments, so use snapshot reads or an accrual journal fed by postings (Ch 21).

193
00:15:22.531 --> 00:15:24.920
Mule accounts and transaction monitoring.

194
00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:32.539
Mule accounts receive fraud proceeds from many victims and forward them within minutes to collector accounts before cash-out.

195
00:15:32.819 --> 00:15:35.997
Legitimate accounts look similar on any single feature:

196
00:15:37.347 --> 00:15:38.713
Here is a quick table:

197
00:15:38.993 --> 00:15:40.806
Feature, and Also seen in.

198
00:15:41.086 --> 00:15:42.067
Many payers:

199
00:15:42.347 --> 00:15:43.179
Shops.

200
00:15:43.459 --> 00:15:44.611
Fast pass-through:

201
00:15:44.891 --> 00:15:46.427
Salary followed by rent.

202
00:15:46.707 --> 00:15:48.542
Many payers, then one payment:

203
00:15:48.822 --> 00:15:50.678
Group collections paid to a vendor.

204
00:15:52.028 --> 00:15:53.799
L4 on synthetic data:

205
00:15:54.079 --> 00:15:59.924
Single-feature rules flag thousands of accounts, at 0.5 to 2% precision.

206
00:16:00.204 --> 00:16:14.476
Requiring fan-in, pass-through, few receivers and a meaningful amount in the same 2-hour window, excluding payments to registered merchants, reaches 98.5% precision and finds 67 of 80 mules.

207
00:16:14.756 --> 00:16:22.031
Following the money (accounts fed by ≥ 2 flagged mules) finds 23 of 24 collectors.

208
00:16:22.311 --> 00:16:23.292
In production:

209
00:16:24.642 --> 00:16:46.701
features are computed in streaming windows (Ch 21, Ch 51);. graph features are refreshed frequently;. a model is trained on labelled cases;. actions are graded: monitor, then a credit hold, then a debit freeze, subject to legal process (VERIFY);. suspicious transaction reporting follows regulatory requirements (VERIFY).

210
00:16:48.051 --> 00:16:58.696
Real-time controls at payment time: device binding, new-device cooling limits, per-transaction and daily limits, and beneficiary risk (VERIFY current UPI limits).

211
00:17:00.046 --> 00:17:03.331
24 times 7 banking and the end of end of day.

212
00:17:03.611 --> 00:17:08.326
Traditional core banking systems ran EOD batches that paused transactions.

213
00:17:08.606 --> 00:17:10.889
Instant payments made that unacceptable.

214
00:17:11.169 --> 00:17:12.107
Approaches:

215
00:17:12.387 --> 00:17:17.230
Separate the payment ledger (always on) from batch processes that read snapshots.

216
00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:23.547
Business date versus calendar date: transactions after the cut-over belong to the next business date.

217
00:17:23.827 --> 00:17:27.241
Accrual and charges as streaming jobs over postings.

218
00:17:28.591 --> 00:17:38.937
Stand-in processing during core maintenance: a shadow ledger authorises within limits, and postings are replayed later (VERIFY applicability per product and regulator).

219
00:17:39.217 --> 00:17:52.572
Modernising a legacy core is a strangler migration (Ch 16): a payments hub in front of the old core;. new products on a new ledger;. accounts migrated in cohorts, with dual-run reconciliation.

220
00:17:53.922 --> 00:17:56.482
Resilience, residency and regulators.

221
