306
00:23:28.506 --> 00:23:30.490
L1, The hot merchant account.

307
00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:39.005
A large merchant's settlement account receives thousands of UPI credits per second. while refunds (debits) must never overdraw it.

308
00:23:39.285 --> 00:23:44.938
Real PostgreSQL 16, fsync on, 16 client threads, 5 s per run.

309
00:23:45.218 --> 00:23:50.871
A: one balance row, UPDATE per credit or debit (debit guarded by balance = amount).

310
00:23:53.721 --> 00:24:01.913
On screen: one merchant account, 16 threads, 5 s per run, credits 1 rupees, 2,000; fsync on. normal day:

311
00:24:02.193 --> 00:24:11.345
5% debits (refunds, payouts). design txn per second p50 ms p99 ms refused errors invariant.

312
00:24:11.625 --> 00:24:19.775
A single balance row 1,585 6.65 48.27 0 0 holds.

313
00:24:20.055 --> 00:24:30.188
B journal + roll-up every 200 ms 5,687 1.38 31.90 0 0 holds.

314
00:24:30.468 --> 00:24:40.601
C 16 sub-account rows 3,932 1.44 41.00 0 0 holds. refund storm:

315
00:24:40.881 --> 00:24:49.991
30% debits (refunds, payouts). design txn per second p50 ms p99 ms refused errors invariant.

316
00:24:50.271 --> 00:24:58.591
A single balance row 1,741 6.26 41.02 0 0 holds.

317
00:24:58.871 --> 00:25:08.663
B journal + roll-up every 200 ms 2,446 0.85 30.16 0 0 holds.

318
00:25:08.943 --> 00:25:17.689
C 16 sub-account rows 2,449 1.18 25.86 0 0 holds.

319
00:25:20.539 --> 00:25:21.393
Reading it:

320
00:25:21.673 --> 00:25:32.745
A single balance row caps out at ~1,600 txn per second, with p50 6.7 ms, because every transaction queues on one row lock.

321
00:25:33.025 --> 00:25:42.198
Journal + roll-up: credits become plain inserts, giving 5,687 per second, with p50 1.4 ms.

322
00:25:42.478 --> 00:25:45.273
The balance is corrected every 200 ms.

323
00:25:45.553 --> 00:25:50.075
Sub-accounts spread the lock and reach 3,932 per second.

324
00:25:50.355 --> 00:25:57.417
In a refund storm (30% debits), both designs fall to ~2,450 per second.

325
00:25:57.697 --> 00:26:03.222
Debits must see the whole balance, so they serialise on the account lock whatever you do to credits.

326
00:26:04.572 --> 00:26:11.335
The invariant held in every run: final balance = opening + credits minus debits, never negative.

327
00:26:11.615 --> 00:26:16.457
The check constraint, the debit lock and the effective-balance check guarantee it together.

328
00:26:16.737 --> 00:26:21.516
A design note from building it: the first roll-up remembered rolled up to sequence N.

329
00:26:21.796 --> 00:26:27.940
A lower sequence can still be uncommitted when a higher one commits, so that version could skip credits forever.

330
00:26:28.220 --> 00:26:32.124
Marking rows rolled in one statement sees only committed rows.

331
00:26:32.404 --> 00:26:35.817
Timings vary about ±10% between runs.

332
