464
00:38:35.365 --> 00:38:39.013
Snapshot-based batches, business dates (§2.9).

465
00:38:40.363 --> 00:38:41.451
Observability.

466
00:38:41.731 --> 00:38:46.403
PENDING count and age per counterparty bank, with the time to TAT deadline.

467
00:38:46.683 --> 00:38:47.941
Status-check rate.

468
00:38:48.221 --> 00:38:49.949
Roll-up lag for hot accounts.

469
00:38:50.229 --> 00:38:54.176
GL balancing checks (debits = credits) every few minutes.

470
00:38:54.456 --> 00:38:56.461
Accrual totals versus expected.

471
00:38:56.741 --> 00:38:58.981
Mule alert volumes and their outcomes.

472
00:39:00.331 --> 00:39:01.270
Security.

473
00:39:01.550 --> 00:39:06.819
Device binding and SIM checks for UPI apps (at the PSP; VERIFY).

474
00:39:07.099 --> 00:39:08.507
HSM-backed keys.

475
00:39:08.787 --> 00:39:10.921
Maker-checker for manual postings.

476
00:39:11.201 --> 00:39:15.595
Least privilege and full audit for operations staff (Ch 23).

477
00:39:16.945 --> 00:39:18.289
Performance and cost.

478
00:39:18.569 --> 00:39:22.452
The journal pattern moves hot-row contention to insert throughput.

479
00:39:22.732 --> 00:39:24.929
Status-check budgets protect the switch.

480
00:39:25.209 --> 00:39:28.559
Batches run on replicas or snapshots, off the primary.

481
00:39:29.909 --> 00:39:30.805
Compliance.

482
00:39:31.085 --> 00:39:32.109
KYC or AML.

483
00:39:32.389 --> 00:39:37.210
RBI directions on payment data storage, outsourcing and IT governance.

484
00:39:37.490 --> 00:39:39.346
TAT and compensation.

485
00:39:39.626 --> 00:39:42.698
Customer protection for unauthorised transactions.

486
00:39:42.978 --> 00:39:45.538
Reporting to the financial intelligence unit.

487
00:39:45.818 --> 00:39:50.405
All of these are VERIFY items against current texts (Ch 36).

488
00:39:51.755 --> 00:39:52.693
Part 7.

489
00:39:52.973 --> 00:39:54.680
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

490
00:39:55.930 --> 00:39:56.847
Watch out:

491
00:39:57.127 --> 00:39:57.767
1.

492
00:39:58.047 --> 00:40:00.778
Updating balances in place without postings.

493
00:40:01.058 --> 00:40:03.810
There is no audit trail and no way to reconcile.

494
00:40:05.160 --> 00:40:06.077
Watch out:

495
00:40:06.357 --> 00:40:07.061
2.

496
00:40:07.341 --> 00:40:09.965
One hot balance row for a large merchant.

497
00:40:10.245 --> 00:40:12.549
It serialises every credit (L1).

498
00:40:13.899 --> 00:40:14.817
Watch out:

499
00:40:15.097 --> 00:40:15.843
3.

500
00:40:16.123 --> 00:40:18.299
Rolling up by sequence high-water mark.

501
00:40:18.579 --> 00:40:20.158
Credits in flight are skipped.

502
00:40:21.508 --> 00:40:22.425
Watch out:

503
00:40:22.705 --> 00:40:23.409
4.

504
00:40:23.689 --> 00:40:25.268
Auto-reversing on timeout.

505
00:40:25.548 --> 00:40:27.127
Double payments (L2).

506
00:40:28.477 --> 00:40:29.394
Watch out:

507
00:40:29.674 --> 00:40:30.442
5.

508
00:40:30.722 --> 00:40:32.407
Treating timeouts as success.

509
00:40:32.687 --> 00:40:34.501
TAT breaches (L2).

510
00:40:35.851 --> 00:40:36.768
Watch out:

511
00:40:37.048 --> 00:40:37.795
6.

512
00:40:38.075 --> 00:40:40.059
Unbounded status-check retries.

513
00:40:40.339 --> 00:40:42.323
They storm the switch after an incident.

514
00:40:43.673 --> 00:40:44.590
Watch out:

515
00:40:44.870 --> 00:40:45.617
7.

516
00:40:45.897 --> 00:40:47.305
Floating point for money.

517
00:40:47.585 --> 00:40:49.035
Always use decimals.

518
00:40:50.385 --> 00:40:51.303
Watch out:

519
00:40:51.583 --> 00:40:52.265
8.

520
00:40:52.545 --> 00:40:55.404
Rounding interest daily when the terms say otherwise.

521
00:40:55.684 --> 00:40:57.412
Per-account errors (L3).

522
00:40:58.762 --> 00:40:59.679
Watch out:

523
00:40:59.959 --> 00:41:00.706
9.

524
00:41:00.986 --> 00:41:03.482
Hard-coding 365 days.

525
00:41:03.762 --> 00:41:05.575
Leap years overpay (L3).

526
00:41:06.925 --> 00:41:07.843
Watch out:

527
00:41:08.123 --> 00:41:08.827
10.

528
00:41:09.107 --> 00:41:10.813
Single-feature fraud rules.

529
00:41:11.093 --> 00:41:13.184
Thousands of false alerts (L4).

530
00:41:14.534 --> 00:41:15.451
Watch out:

531
00:41:15.731 --> 00:41:16.563
11.

532
00:41:16.843 --> 00:41:18.827
EOD batches that stop payments.

533
00:41:19.107 --> 00:41:21.838
Unacceptable for 24 times 7 rails.

534
00:41:23.188 --> 00:41:24.105
Watch out:

535
00:41:24.385 --> 00:41:25.175
12.

536
00:41:25.455 --> 00:41:27.268
Unaudited manual corrections.

537
00:41:27.548 --> 00:41:28.657
Insider risk.

538
00:41:30.007 --> 00:41:30.882
Part 8.

539
00:41:31.162 --> 00:41:32.506
Interview Q and A.

540
00:41:33.756 --> 00:41:34.588
Beginner.

541
00:41:34.868 --> 00:41:35.892
Question 1.

542
00:41:36.172 --> 00:41:39.308
What is double-entry bookkeeping and why do banks use it?

543
00:41:39.588 --> 00:41:40.953
Thirty-second answer:

544
00:41:41.233 --> 00:41:54.417
Every transaction is recorded as equal debits and credits across accounts, so money is only ever moved, never created or lost; the books always balance, which makes errors detectable and audits possible.

545
00:41:55.767 --> 00:41:56.770
Question 2.

546
00:41:57.050 --> 00:42:00.506
What is the difference between ledger balance and available balance?

547
00:42:00.786 --> 00:42:02.151
Thirty-second answer:

548
00:42:02.431 --> 00:42:14.890
Ledger is the sum of posted entries; available is ledger minus holds (pending debits like card authorisations or pending UPI) plus any overdraft limit, what you can actually spend now.

549
00:42:16.240 --> 00:42:17.285
Question 3.

550
00:42:17.565 --> 00:42:20.211
Walk through a UPI payment at a high level.

551
00:42:20.491 --> 00:42:21.856
Thirty-second answer:

552
00:42:22.136 --> 00:42:35.875
The payer approves with a PIN in their UPI app; the switch asks the payer's bank to debit, then the beneficiary's bank to credit; both respond and the apps show success; banks settle net amounts in cycles later.

553
00:42:37.225 --> 00:42:38.291
Question 4.

554
00:42:38.571 --> 00:42:41.217
Why must banks never use floating point for money?

555
00:42:41.497 --> 00:42:42.862
Thirty-second answer:

556
00:42:43.142 --> 00:42:57.435
Binary floating point can't represent most decimal fractions exactly, so sums drift by fractions of a paisa and comparisons fail; banks use fixed-point integers (paise) or decimal types with explicit rounding rules.

557
00:42:58.785 --> 00:42:59.788
Intermediate.

558
00:43:00.068 --> 00:43:01.177
Question 5.

559
00:43:01.457 --> 00:43:03.441
How do you make postings idempotent?

560
00:43:03.721 --> 00:43:05.087
Thirty-second answer:

561
00:43:05.367 --> 00:43:21.196
Every posting carries the upstream reference (UPI transaction id, request id) with a unique constraint; a retry finds the existing posting and returns its result; reversals reference the original posting and are unique too.

562
00:43:22.546 --> 00:43:23.655
Question 6.

563
00:43:23.935 --> 00:43:26.218
What is a hold (lien) and when is it used?

564
00:43:26.498 --> 00:43:27.863
Thirty-second answer:

565
00:43:28.143 --> 00:43:42.458
A reservation that reduces available balance without posting, for card authorisations, pending UPI debits, legal orders or collateral; it is converted into a posting or released, with an expiry and a reason.

566
00:43:43.808 --> 00:43:44.896
Question 7.

567
00:43:45.176 --> 00:43:49.571
What happens in end-of-day processing, and why is it a problem for UPI?

568
00:43:49.851 --> 00:43:51.216
Thirty-second answer:

569
00:43:51.496 --> 00:44:08.392
Accrual, charges, standing instructions, statements and GL balancing on a closed business date, traditionally blocking transactions; UPI is 24 times 7, so batches must run on snapshots or streams with business dates, never pausing payments.

570
00:44:09.742 --> 00:44:10.766
Question 8.

571
00:44:11.046 --> 00:44:15.163
How do settlement and reconciliation work between banks for UPI?

572
00:44:15.443 --> 00:44:16.809
Thirty-second answer:

573
00:44:17.089 --> 00:44:33.153
Transactions are cleared in cycles; the switch computes net positions per bank and settles through accounts at the central bank; each bank reconciles its own records against the settlement files and resolves mismatches, the files are the final truth for outcomes.

574
00:44:34.503 --> 00:44:35.569
Question 9.

575
00:44:35.849 --> 00:44:38.132
How do you compute savings interest correctly?

576
00:44:38.412 --> 00:44:39.777
Thirty-second answer:

577
00:44:40.057 --> 00:44:55.375
Follow the product terms: daily balances on value dates, the specified day-count convention, high-precision accrual, a single rounding at posting with the specified mode, credited at the specified frequency, and test against worked examples.

578
00:44:56.725 --> 00:44:57.493
Senior.

579
00:44:57.773 --> 00:44:58.818
Question 10.

580
00:44:59.098 --> 00:45:02.490
A merchant account receives 3,000 credits per second.

581
00:45:02.770 --> 00:45:04.370
How do you design its balance?

582
00:45:04.650 --> 00:45:06.015
Thirty-second answer:

583
00:45:06.295 --> 00:45:23.362
Credits become inserts into an append-only journal (or spread over sub-accounts); a roll-up folds committed rows into the balance frequently; debits take a per-account lock and check balance plus unrolled rows; roll-up marks rows rather than using a sequence high-water mark.

584
00:45:24.712 --> 00:45:25.843
Question 11.

585
00:45:26.123 --> 00:45:29.003
The credit leg of a UPI payment times out.

586
00:45:29.283 --> 00:45:30.840
What does the remitter bank do?

587
00:45:31.120 --> 00:45:32.485
Thirty-second answer:

588
00:45:32.765 --> 00:45:50.664
Keep the debit in PENDING, neither reverse nor finalise; run jittered status checks with backoff; resolve from status or settlement files, whichever comes first; reverse at the next settlement cycle if still unknown, well before the TAT deadline; tell the customer it's processing.

589
00:45:52.014 --> 00:45:53.102
Question 12.

590
00:45:53.382 --> 00:45:55.153
How would you detect mule accounts?

591
00:45:55.433 --> 00:45:56.798
Thirty-second answer:

592
00:45:57.078 --> 00:46:14.358
Combine features in the same short window, fan-in from many distinct senders, rapid pass-through, few receivers, meaningful amounts, excluding payments to registered merchants, then follow the money to accounts fed by several suspects; score, review, and act proportionately.

593
00:46:15.708 --> 00:46:16.988
Question 13.

594
00:46:17.268 --> 00:46:21.897
How do you run interest accrual for 50 million accounts without stopping payments?

595
00:46:22.177 --> 00:46:23.543
Thirty-second answer:

596
00:46:23.823 --> 00:46:39.055
Read a consistent snapshot of value-dated balances (replica or snapshot export), or maintain accrual incrementally from the posting stream; post accruals as balanced entries under the business date; reconcile totals against the GL.

597
00:46:40.405 --> 00:46:41.599
Question 14.

598
00:46:41.879 --> 00:46:45.634
What makes a bank's DR design different from a typical web service?

599
00:46:45.914 --> 00:46:47.279
Thirty-second answer:

600
00:46:47.559 --> 00:47:03.581
Near-zero RPO for the ledger (you cannot lose postings), regulator expectations on recovery and drills, data localisation limiting where DR can be, and reconciliation after failover to prove no posting was lost or duplicated.

601
00:47:04.931 --> 00:47:05.827
Architect.

602
00:47:06.107 --> 00:47:07.301
Question 15.

603
00:47:07.581 --> 00:47:10.483
How would you modernise a legacy core banking system?

604
00:47:10.763 --> 00:47:12.128
Thirty-second answer:

605
00:47:12.408 --> 00:47:30.413
Strangler approach: a payments hub and API layer in front of the old core, new products on a new ledger, migrate account cohorts with dual-run reconciliation, move batch work to streams, and retire old modules only after sustained parity, with regulator engagement throughout.

606
00:47:31.763 --> 00:47:32.958
Question 16.

607
00:47:33.238 --> 00:47:36.267
How do regulatory deadlines shape system design?

608
00:47:36.547 --> 00:47:37.913
Thirty-second answer:

609
00:47:38.193 --> 00:47:55.153
They become explicit timers in state machines (TAT for reversals, reporting windows, dispute timelines), with dashboards of the oldest item per deadline, alerts ahead of breach, and automatic safe actions at the deadline, plus audit evidence that the timers ran.

610
00:47:56.503 --> 00:47:57.847
Question 17.

611
00:47:58.127 --> 00:48:01.113
Design the risk controls for a bank's UPI payments.

612
00:48:01.393 --> 00:48:02.759
Thirty-second answer:

613
00:48:03.039 --> 00:48:21.833
Device and SIM binding at the PSP, limits per transaction per day and for new devices or beneficiaries, real-time scoring using velocity and graph features, beneficiary mule scores, step-up or decline actions, and post-transaction monitoring with case management and reporting.

614
00:48:23.183 --> 00:48:24.357
Question 18.

615
00:48:24.637 --> 00:48:28.541
How would you ensure a bank's ledger is always balanced and proven so?

616
00:48:28.821 --> 00:48:30.186
Thirty-second answer:

617
00:48:30.466 --> 00:48:48.578
Enforce balanced postings at write time (constraint or transactional check), immutable entries, continuous GL balancing jobs, reconciliation with external statements and settlement files, and independent audits of the tooling, alerting on any non-zero imbalance immediately.

618
00:48:49.928 --> 00:48:51.165
Question 19.

619
00:48:51.445 --> 00:48:54.923
What are the design implications of payment data localisation?

620
00:48:55.203 --> 00:48:56.568
Thirty-second answer:

621
00:48:56.848 --> 00:49:15.984
Payment system data must be stored in the country (per the regulator's directive), which constrains cloud regions, DR sites, analytics and vendors; processing abroad may have conditions; design data classification and regional storage from the start (VERIFY current RBI rules and FAQs).

622
00:49:17.334 --> 00:49:18.443
Question 20.

623
00:49:18.723 --> 00:49:23.502
Compare a merchant's payment system (Ch 45) with a bank's core ledger.

624
00:49:23.782 --> 00:49:25.147
Thirty-second answer:

625
00:49:25.427 --> 00:49:45.310
The merchant integrates gateways and reconciles against them, owning order state and a ledger of receivables; the bank owns the actual accounts, holds, interest and regulatory deadlines, participates in the switch and settlement, and must never create money, both need idempotency, state machines and reconciliation.

626
00:49:46.660 --> 00:49:47.556
Part 9.

627
00:49:47.836 --> 00:49:49.244
Summary and Practice.

628
00:49:50.494 --> 00:49:51.582
Key takeaways.

629
00:49:51.862 --> 00:49:55.979
Double entry, immutable postings and holds are the bank's data model.

630
00:49:56.259 --> 00:50:03.150
Hot accounts: credits go to an append-only journal, debits check under a lock, and the roll-up marks rows (L1).

631
00:50:03.430 --> 00:50:10.086
Unknown outcomes stay PENDING until resolved by status checks or settlement, before the TAT (L2).

632
00:50:10.366 --> 00:50:16.297
Interest follows the terms: decimal arithmetic, single rounding, the correct day count (L3).

633
00:50:16.577 --> 00:50:20.758
Detect mules with windowed combinations and graph features (L4).

634
00:50:22.108 --> 00:50:23.089
Cheat sheet.

635
00:50:23.369 --> 00:50:24.735
Here is a quick table:

